# Code owners. GitHub requests a review from the owner of every file a pull
# request touches, and the last matching pattern in this file wins.
#
# For a repository with one maintainer this is close to a no-op, and it is here
# anyway for the day it stops being one. The point is not the blanket rule at
# the top; it is the four paths below it, which are the ones where an accepted
# change is difficult to take back.
#
# ## Why a username and not a team
#
# This file named `@Firelight-Innovations/maintainers` until 2026-08-21, and
# that team does not exist — the organisation has no teams at all. **A
# CODEOWNERS rule naming an unresolvable owner matches nobody**, and GitHub does
# not fail the file or warn in the pull request, so every rule below was silently
# doing nothing for as long as it had been there.
#
# A username cannot be wrong in that way. Swap it back to a team when one exists
# and has members; until then a real owner beats a tidier-looking one.
#
# ## This only bites once review is required
#
# CODEOWNERS on its own *requests* a review. It blocks nothing until
# "Require review from Code Owners" is on in `main`'s branch protection, which
# it is not as of 2026-08-21 — `required_pull_request_reviews` is null.
#
# That setting is what makes this file a boundary rather than a convention, and
# it is the only boundary GitHub evaluates server-side. It matters most for
# `/.github/`: a `pull_request` workflow runs the pull request's own code, so a
# check living in this tree — `scripts/check-catalog.mjs`, say — can be edited
# by the very change it is checking. Nothing inside a branch can reach this
# rule, which is the whole point of it.

