# Schematify's design/audit split (docs/design/SCHEMATIFY-PRD.md §14.6, §6.2).
# `nodes/`, `edges/`, `rules/`, `screens/`, `flows/`, `decisions/`, `registry/`
# and `brief.json` are semantic — human-authored design, the thing an agent
# "shall not change" per §6.2. `runs/` and `layout/` are the two trees a machine
# writes instead: a CI job appends a benchmark result under `runs/`, and a node
# drag rewrites `layout/`. §14.6 is explicit that those two carry no owner "because
# a CI job and a node drag write them" — assigning a human owner to a tree only
# automation touches would make every routine write wait on a review it does not
# need. Section 6.3's one exception, the lifecycle pair, is a human-triggered
# transition and is still `nodes/` + `runs/`; it is `nodes/` that carries the
# owner in that pair, same as any other write there, and `runs/` still does not.
#
# No leading slash on any of the lines below, deliberately — unlike every rule
# above. `.veistra/` is a *project's* root, not a fixed location in this repository:
# today it exists three times, under
# `crates/schematify-core/fixtures/{dense-service,saas-backend,stress-2000}/`,
# and a project opened elsewhere in a checkout (including at the repository
# root, if this repository is ever opened as a Schematify project itself) would
# add another. An unanchored pattern matches `.veistra/nodes/` at any depth, the
# same way `apps/ @octocat` in GitHub's own CODEOWNERS documentation matches an
# `apps` directory anywhere rather than only at the root — so this does not need
# updating every time a fixture is added or renamed.
#
# The lines below come after `/crates/` above on purpose: CODEOWNERS resolves
# a path against the *last* matching line in the file, and `/crates/` alone
# would hand every one of these fixture trees to the owner, `runs/` and
# `layout/` included. Placing the two unowned lines last gives them the final
# word for the paths they name.
